; host/refs.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Refs`.
;
; `BeamLisp.Refs` is beam-lisp's Clojure-shaped reference runtime: atoms
; (`deref`/`swap!`/`reset!`/`compare-and-set!` plus `add-watch!`/`remove-watch!`),
; volatiles (a process-dictionary box), promises (a single-assignment cell
; backed by a small process), futures (a running `Task`), and the `deref` that
; reads all of them — including the two value-shaped references that live
; ELSEWHERE in the pinned floor, `BeamLisp.Delay` and `BeamLisp.Derived`.
;
; This is the `.bl` port of `lib/beam_lisp/refs.ex`'s `BeamLisp.Refs` half,
; compiled by `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into ONE exact-named beam
; (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Refs.beam`).
;
; ── THE STRUCT TYPES ARE A SEPARATE WAVE (and why this file needs none) ────
;
; `refs.ex` declares six modules, but only ONE of them carries behaviour. The
; other five are bare `defstruct` types:
;
;     defstruct [:cell, :watches]   ; Atom
;     defstruct [:key]              ; Volatile
;     defstruct [:value]            ; Reduced
;     defstruct [:pid]              ; Promise
;     defstruct [:task]             ; Future
;
; A bare `defstruct` generates exactly `__struct__/0,1` and `__info__/1` and
; NOTHING else, and `compile_host_file` REFUSES a source with no top-level
; `defn` while `:struct_fields` may not be combined with a hand-written
; `__struct__`/`__info__` (it refuses the collision). So there is exactly one
; honest shape for each of them — spell the generated surface out by hand, the
; technique `priv/host/sorted_map.bl` documents for `SortedMap`'s
; `defstruct [:tree]` — and it is PER TYPE, because that hand-written surface
; must spell that type's own field names. No two of the five share a field list,
; so no two can share a source: five sources, one per exact atom. That is a
; separate slice of work (a port wave of its own), and this file is deliberately
; INDEPENDENT of it.
;
; Independence is possible because a struct is only ever a TAGGED MAP: the tag
; is the `:"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom"` atom, and nothing needs `BeamLisp.Atom` to be
; LOADED to build or read one. So — exactly as `priv/host/reactive.bl` spells
; `:"Elixir.BeamLisp.Derived"` and `priv/host/deferred.bl` spells
; `:"Elixir.BeamLisp.Delay"` — every type tag here is spelled LITERALLY and
; every value is built with `maps/put` over `(maps/new)`. No `%Mod{…}` literal is
; used, so nothing is required of the compiler's struct knowledge either, and
; the five type beams can be pinned in any order relative to this one.
;
; ── WHY EVERY READ IS `erlang/map_get`, NEVER `(:field x)` ─────────────────
;
; beam-lisp's `get`/`seq`/`keys` route through the runtime's collection access,
; and `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` classifies `BeamLisp.Atom`, `Volatile`, `Promise`,
; `Future`, `Delay` and `Derived` as REFERENCE-CELL kinds — for those, a
; collection read is REFUSED (`get: BeamLisp.Atom is a reference, not a
; collection`). The recalled Elixir module pattern-matched and used `atom.cell`,
; which is a raw map read; so is `erlang/map_get/2`. `tag/1` is the one guarded
; read: a non-map has no tag, and `maps/get/3` would raise on one.
;
; ── WHAT THE PINNED PORT OWES ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ABI parity is the contract: the same answers, the same VALUE shapes (a real
; `%BeamLisp.Atom{}`-tagged map, a real `Promise` pid, a real `Task`), the same
; exception CLASSES AND MESSAGES, and the same PROCESS SEMANTICS —
;
;   * `swap!`/`reset!`/`compare-and-set!` run the caller's fn IN THE CALLER's
;     process and notify watches IN THE CALLER's process, after the commit;
;   * `future_exec` runs the thunk in a `Task` that INHERITS the spawning env;
;   * `promise/0`'s loop runs in a fresh process that inherits it too, and
;     `deref` registers as a waiter (no polling);
;   * `deref` of a `Delay`/`Derived` delegates to the pinned `Deferred`/
;     `Reactive` modules, so the already-seeded native pair answers here.
;
; `deref` reads a Delay/Derived through the PUBLIC tag, never through the
; struct module: `(BeamLisp.Deferred/force x)` and `(BeamLisp.Reactive/deref x)`
; are calls to modules that ARE on the floor (they are seeded pairs), and they
; read the same tagged maps this file would.
;
; ── THE ONE EXPORT SUPERSET ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A pinned module has NO PRIVATE SURFACE: the emitter exports every top-level
; `defn`, and `defn-` is not privatized on this path (the same divergence
; `priv/host/vector.bl` documents). `refs.ex`'s four `defp`s — `update_watches/2`,
; `notify_watches/3`, `apply_heap_bound/0` and `promise_loop/2` — ride along as
; exports here, along with `tag/1`, `rethrow/1` and `timeout-exit?/1`. Every
; export the live module serves, this beam serves; the beam is a SUPERSET by
; exactly those. Nothing calls them from outside; they are the shared internals
; written once rather than duplicated per call site.
;
; ── BOOT SAFETY: NO HOST-NAMESPACE VAR IN THE PATH ────────────────────────
;
; `str` and `cons` are RT PRIMS — host-namespace vars — so a message is joined
; with the raw `erlang/iolist_to_binary` (every piece is already a binary: the
; literals and `Kernel/inspect`'s answer) and a list is grown with `erlang/++`.
; That is the discipline `priv/host/atom_guard.bl` documents ("It is NOT `str`"):
; a pinned beam must not resolve through `BeamLisp.Env` for something it can ask
; the runtime for. Field reads are `erlang/map_get`, never `(:field x)` — a
; keyword read is a COLLECTION read, which RT refuses on a reference-typed
; struct. Everything else here is a remote call to `:erlang`/`:maps`/an Elixir
; module, a guard, a language form, or an in-file helper in call position.
;
; ── THE ONE PLACE THE PORT CANNOT BE LITERAL ──────────────────────────────
;
; `deref/3` on a future is `try Task.await(task, ms) catch :exit, {:timeout, _}
; -> timeout_val`. beam-lisp's `catch` has no class-typed form (it binds one
; normalized value: `{:exit, reason}` for an exit), and its `:erlang.raise/3`
; is the raw primop that segfaults on a frame list — so the caught exit is
; re-raised by CLASS from the normalized value (`:exit` → `erlang/exit`,
; `:throw` → `throw`, anything else as an error). Class and reason are
; preserved exactly; the stack is the re-raise's own, which is all a caller can
; observe anyway (the live catch swallows the timeout's stack too). Nothing else
; in this file needs a class-typed catch.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_refs_test.bl

(ns host.refs)

; ── small internals ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Not private — see "the one export superset" above.

(defn- tag
  "The `:__struct__` tag of a map, else nil. Read with `maps/get/3` and guarded
   by `is_map`, never `(:__struct__ x)`: beam-lisp's keyword lookup is a
   COLLECTION read, and RT refuses one on a reference-typed struct."
  [x]
  (if (erlang/is_map x) (maps/get :__struct__ x nil) nil))

(defn- update_watches
  "CAS-loop on a watches cell, so concurrent add/remove do not lose each
   other. `f` maps the old table to the new one."
  [cell f]
  (let [old (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read cell)
        outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_ref cell old (f old))]
    (cond
      (erlang/== :ok outcome) :ok
      (erlang/== :retry outcome) (update_watches cell f)
      :else
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception "watch table would form a reference cycle")))))

(defn- notify_watches
  "Call every watch as `(f key ref old new)`, in the CALLER's process, after the
   value has committed. A no-op change still notifies — the watch decides what
   counts as a change, not the atom (Clojure's contract)."
  [x old new]
  (let [watches (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :watches x))]
    (if (erlang/== 0 (erlang/map_size watches))
      :ok
      (Enum/each watches
        (fn [pair]
          (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke (erlang/element 2 pair)
            (list (erlang/element 1 pair) x old new)))))))

(defn- apply_heap_bound
  "Set this process's `:max_heap_size` to the caller's env bound, if it has one.
   OTP 29 treats `kill: false` as a soft warning, not a resource bound.
   Enforce the hard limit; `future_exec` unlinks bounded tasks before they can
   allocate, so a heap kill cannot take the caller down through the Task link."
  []
  (let [words (BeamLisp.Env/max_heap)]
    (if (erlang/== nil words)
      :ok
      (erlang/process_flag :max_heap_size
        {:size words :kill true :error_logger false}))))

(defn- rethrow
  "Re-raise a value a beam-lisp `catch` bound. beam-lisp normalizes what it
   catches (`{:exit, reason}` for an exit, `{:throw, value}` for a throw, the
   normalized exception struct for an error), and has no class-typed catch — so
   the class is recovered here and raised again. See the header."
  [e]
  (if (erlang/is_map e)
    (erlang/error e)
    (if (and (erlang/is_tuple e) (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size e)))
      (cond
        (erlang/== :exit (erlang/element 1 e)) (erlang/exit (erlang/element 2 e))
        (erlang/== :throw (erlang/element 1 e)) (throw (erlang/element 2 e))
        :else (erlang/error e))
      (erlang/error e))))

(defn- timeout-exit?
  "A timed deref returns its default on timeout or a hard heap-limit kill.
   Task.await exits with `{:timeout, _}` or `{:killed, _}` respectively;
   unrelated task failures still propagate."
  [e]
  (and (erlang/is_tuple e)
       (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size e))
       (erlang/== :exit (erlang/element 1 e))
       (erlang/is_tuple (erlang/element 2 e))
       (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size (erlang/element 2 e)))
       (or (erlang/== :timeout (erlang/element 1 (erlang/element 2 e)))
           (erlang/== :killed (erlang/element 1 (erlang/element 2 e))))))

; ── atoms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn atom
  "A new atom holding `v`: one GC-owned cell for the value, a SECOND cell for
   the watch table, and the `BeamLisp.Atom` tag. Two cells, not one map, so a
   watch table is never mistaken for part of the value."
  [v]
  (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom"
    (maps/put :cell (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create_ref v)
      (maps/put :watches (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create_ref (maps/new)) (maps/new)))))

(defn swap!
  "Swap the atom's value: `new = (f old & args)`, published with a
   compare-exchange retry loop, and watches notified after the commit IN THE
   CALLER's process. Returns `new`."
  ([ref f] (swap! ref f (list)))
  ([x f args]
   (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
     (let [cell (erlang/map_get :cell x)
           old (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read cell)
           new (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke f (erlang/++ (list old) args))
           outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_ref cell old new)]
       (cond
         (erlang/== :ok outcome)
         (do (notify_watches x old new) new)

         ; A concurrent writer moved the cell: re-read and re-apply — Clojure's
         ; atom contract, and the reason the step fn must be pure.
         (erlang/== :retry outcome)
         (swap! x f args)

         :else
         (erlang/error
           (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
             "swap!: the new value would form a reference cycle"))))
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
         (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "swap!: not a reference: " (Kernel/inspect x))))))))))

(defn reset!
  "Set the atom's value to `v` (compare-exchange retry loop), notifying watches
   after the commit. Returns `v`."
  [x v]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (let [cell (erlang/map_get :cell x)
          old (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read cell)
          outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_ref cell old v)]
      (cond
        (erlang/== :ok outcome) (do (notify_watches x old v) v)
        (erlang/== :retry outcome) (reset! x v)
        :else
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
            "reset!: the value would form a reference cycle"))))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "reset!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

(defn add_watch!
  "Register `f` under `key`; it is called `(f key ref old new)` on change.
   Returns the atom."
  [x key f]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (do (update_watches (erlang/map_get :watches x) (fn [m] (maps/put key f m))) x)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "add-watch!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

(defn remove_watch!
  "Remove the watch registered under `key`. Returns the atom."
  [x key]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (do (update_watches (erlang/map_get :watches x) (fn [m] (maps/remove key m))) x)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "remove-watch!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

(defn compare_and_set!
  "Atomically set to `new` iff the current value is `old` (VALUE equality —
   Clojure `=`, which is beam-lisp `==`, so `1` and `1.0` count as equal).
   Returns `true` on commit (`false` when the current value differs); notifies
   watches on commit."
  [x old new]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (let [cell (erlang/map_get :cell x)
          current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read cell)]
      ; Check value equality against the current term, then exchange against
      ; THAT exact term: a racing writer changes the term, the exact exchange
      ; answers `:retry`, and we re-check.
      (if (erlang/== current old)
        (let [outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_ref cell current new)]
          (cond
            (erlang/== :ok outcome) (do (notify_watches x current new) true)
            (erlang/== :retry outcome) (compare_and_set! x old new)
            :else
            (erlang/error
              (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
                "compare-and-set!: the value would form a reference cycle"))))
        false))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "compare-and-set!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

; ── volatiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; An unsynchronized box in the CREATING process's dictionary, keyed by a unique
; reference — deliberately cheaper than an atom, honouring the deal Clojure's
; volatiles make with stateful transducers (single-threaded by contract). The
; struct holds only the key, never the value, so it is safe to send anywhere;
; the value simply is not visible outside its home process.

(defn volatile
  "A new volatile holding `v`, in this process's dictionary."
  [v]
  (let [key (erlang/make_ref)]
    (Process/put (tuple :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" key) v)
    (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile"
      (maps/put :key key (maps/new)))))

(defn vreset!
  "Overwrite the volatile's value. Returns `v`."
  [x v]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
    (do (Process/put (tuple :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (erlang/map_get :key x)) v) v)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "vreset!: not a volatile: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

(defn vswap!
  "Read-modify-write the volatile, in the caller's process — atomic only
   against other ops in this same process, which is exactly the contract."
  ([vol f] (vswap! vol f (list)))
  ([x f args]
   (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
     (let [key (erlang/map_get :key x)
           new (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke f
                 (erlang/++ (list (Process/get (tuple :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" key))) args))]
       (Process/put (tuple :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" key) new)
       new)
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "vswap!: not a volatile: " (Kernel/inspect x))))))))

(defn volatile?
  "True for a volatile — a map tagged with the `BeamLisp.Volatile` struct."
  [x]
  (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x)))

; ── futures ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn future_exec
  "A `BeamLisp.Future` over a thunk already running in a `Task`. The task
   INHERITS the caller's env binding: pdict does not propagate across spawn, and
   a future that landed in `:global` would silently escape its test's isolated
   env (PLAN-046 L2)."
  [thunk_fn]
  (let [token (BeamLisp.Env/capture)
        bounded? (erlang/not (erlang/== nil (BeamLisp.Env/max_heap)))
        task (Task/async
               (fn []
                 ; Wait until the caller has unlinked a bounded task. A hard
                 ; heap kill before that handshake could kill the caller too.
                 (receive [:bl_future_start] :ok)
                 (BeamLisp.Env/bind token)
                 (apply_heap_bound)
                 (thunk_fn)))]
    (when bounded? (Process/unlink (erlang/map_get :pid task)))
    (erlang/send (erlang/map_get :pid task)
                 (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :bl_future_start)))
    (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future"
      (maps/put :task task (maps/new)))))

(defn spawn_exec
  "A raw process that INHERITS the spawning env — the same binding-conveyance
   `future_exec/1` and `promise/0` use. Raw `:erlang.spawn` starts a child with
   an EMPTY process dict, so the child resolves its vars and caps at `:global`;
   capturing the token here and binding it first makes `spawn` env-transparent.
   `kind` is `:plain | :link | :monitor`, mirroring the three `:erlang.spawn*`
   shapes; `:monitor` returns a `{pid, ref}` tuple as Erlang does."
  ([thunk_fn] (spawn_exec thunk_fn :plain))
  ([thunk_fn kind]
   (let [token (BeamLisp.Env/capture)
         wrapped (fn []
                   (BeamLisp.Env/bind token)
                   (apply_heap_bound)
                   (thunk_fn))]
     (cond
       (erlang/== :plain kind) (erlang/spawn wrapped)
       (erlang/== :link kind) (erlang/spawn_link wrapped)
       (erlang/== :monitor kind) (erlang/spawn_monitor wrapped)
       :else
       (erlang/error (Elixir.CaseClauseError/exception (list (tuple :term kind))))))))

(defn future?
  "True for a future — a map tagged with the `BeamLisp.Future` struct."
  [x]
  (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x)))

(defn future_cancel
  "Cancel a running future with `:brutal_kill`. Answers `true` only when it
   actually cancelled a running computation — `Task.shutdown` returns `nil` (no
   reply) for a task that had already finished."
  [x]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x))
    (let [task (erlang/map_get :task x)
          running (Process/alive? (erlang/map_get :pid task))]
      (Task/shutdown task :brutal_kill)
      running)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "future-cancel: not a future: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

; ── promises ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn promise
  "A single-assignment reference backed by a small process. `deliver` sets a
   value once; `deref` registers as a waiter and is answered when the value
   arrives — no polling anywhere. The loop process inherits the caller's env
   binding, exactly as `future_exec/1` does."
  []
  (let [token (BeamLisp.Env/capture)
        pid (erlang/spawn
              (fn []
                (BeamLisp.Env/bind token)
                (apply_heap_bound)
                (promise_loop :unset (list))))]
    (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise"
      (maps/put :pid pid (maps/new)))))

(defn- promise_loop
  "The promise process: `:unset` (plus a waiter list) or `{:set, v}`. A `deref`
   while unset parks the caller; on `deliver` every waiter is answered (in
   registration order) and the state becomes set, after which later derefs are
   answered immediately and later deliveries are refused. Replies carry this
   process's pid so a timed-out deref can never confuse one promise's value with
   another's."
  [state waiters]
  (receive
    [:bl_deref from]
    (if (erlang/== :unset state)
      (promise_loop state (erlang/++ (list from) waiters))
      (do (erlang/send from (tuple (erlang/self) :bl_value (erlang/element 2 state)))
          (promise_loop state waiters)))

    [:bl_set from v]
    (if (erlang/== :unset state)
      (do (Enum/each (Enum/reverse waiters)
            (fn [w] (erlang/send w (tuple (erlang/self) :bl_value v))))
          (erlang/send from (tuple (erlang/self) :bl_set_ack true))
          (promise_loop (tuple :set v) (list)))
      (do (erlang/send from (tuple (erlang/self) :bl_set_ack false))
          (promise_loop state waiters)))))

(defn deliver
  "Deliver `v` to a promise. Returns the promise on the FIRST delivery, `nil` on
   any later one — Clojure allows a single delivery, and the `nil` return lets
   callers detect the no-op."
  [x v]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" (tag x))
    (let [pid (erlang/map_get :pid x)]
      (erlang/send pid (tuple :bl_set (erlang/self) v))
      (receive
        [ack :bl_set_ack true] :when (== ack pid) x
        [ack :bl_set_ack false] :when (== ack pid) nil))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "deliver: not a promise: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

; ── deref ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn deref
  "Read a derefable reference. Atoms, volatiles and reduceds read immediately;
   a future blocks until its task returns (Clojure's contract); a promise blocks
   until it is delivered; a DELAY forces it once and a DERIVED reads it current,
   so `@d` works on every one of them alike.
   `deref/3` bounds the two BLOCKING cases (future, promise) with a timeout and
   a value to answer instead; the immediate cases ignore both — exactly the
   clause set the recalled module exposed."
  ([x]
   (cond
     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :cell x))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x))
     (Task/await (erlang/map_get :task x) :infinity)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" (tag x))
     (deref x :infinity nil)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
     (Process/get (tuple :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (erlang/map_get :key x)))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Reduced" (tag x))
     (erlang/map_get :value x)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Delay" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.Deferred/force x)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Derived" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.Reactive/deref x)

     :else
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
         (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "deref: not a derefable reference: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

  ([x timeout_ms timeout_val]
   (cond
     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :cell x))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x))
     (try
       (Task/await (erlang/map_get :task x) timeout_ms)
       (catch e
         (if (timeout-exit? e) timeout_val (rethrow e))))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
     (Process/get (tuple :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (erlang/map_get :key x)))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Reduced" (tag x))
     (erlang/map_get :value x)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" (tag x))
     (let [pid (erlang/map_get :pid x)]
       (erlang/send pid (tuple :bl_deref (erlang/self)))
       (receive
         [p :bl_value v] :when (== p pid) v
         (after timeout_ms timeout_val)))

     ; NOTE: deliberately no Delay/Derived clause here — the recalled `deref/3`
     ; had none either, so `(deref d 10 :tv)` on a delay is refused, not a
     ; silent force.
     :else
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
         (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "deref: not a derefable reference: " (Kernel/inspect x))))))))
